{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And they shall make the ephod.</b> If I come to explain the making of the ephod and the breastplate according to the order of the verses, their explanation is lengthy, and the reader will err in putting them together. Therefore I am writing how they are made as they are, so that the reader may run through it, and afterward I shall explain according to the order of the verses. The ephod is made like the kind of apron that women who ride horses wear, and he girds it from behind, opposite his heart, below his armpits. Its width is the measure of a man's back and more, and it reaches down to his heels. And the חֵשֶׁב is joined at its top across its width, the work of a weaver, and it extends to here and to here in order to wrap and gird with it. And the shoulder-pieces are joined to the חֵשֶׁב, one on the right and one on the left, from behind the kohen, at the two ends of the width of the apron. When he lifts them they stand for him upon his two shoulders, and they are like two straps made of the same kind as the ephod, long enough to measure for lifting them at his neck from here and from here, and they fold to his front below his shoulders a little. And the shoham stones are set in them, one on the right shoulder and one on the left. And the מִשְׁבְּצוֹת are placed at their heads in front of his shoulders. And the two cords of gold are inserted into the two rings that are on the breastplate, at its two upper corners, one on the right and one on the left. And the two ends of the chain are fixed in the מִשְׁבֶּצֶת on the right, and likewise the two ends of the left-hand chain are fixed in the מִשְׁבֶּצֶת that is on the left shoulder. Thus the breastplate is suspended from the מִשְׁבְּצוֹת of the ephod over his heart, in front of him. And moreover, two rings at the two ends of the breastplate at its bottom, and opposite them two rings on the two shoulders of the ephod below, at its bottom edge that is joined to the חֵשֶׁב — the rings of the breastplate lie directly opposite the rings of the ephod, one upon the other, and they are fastened with a cord of techelet inserted into the rings of the ephod and the breastplate, so that the bottom of the breastplate is bound to the חֵשֶׁב of the ephod and does not swing and separate, going back and forth." "<b>Gold, techelet, purple, worm-scarlet, and twisted linen.</b> These five kinds are spun into each and every thread. They would hammer the gold into thin plates and cut threads from them, and spin that thread of gold together with six threads of techelet, and a thread of gold with six threads of purple, and likewise with worm-scarlet and likewise with the linen — for all the kinds are a thread folded six-fold, with a thread of gold together with each and every one — and afterward he twists them all as one. Thus the thread is twenty-eight-fold, and so it is explained in tractate Yoma. And it is derived from this verse: ״וְיָרְקְעוּ אֶת פַּחֵי הַזָּהָב וְקִצֵּץ פְּתִילִים לַעֲשׂוֹת״ — ״אֶת פְּתִילֵי הַזָּהָב״ — ״בְּתוֹךְ הַתְּכֵלֶת וּבְתוֹךְ הָאַרְגָּמָן וגוֹ׳״ — we learn that a thread of gold is twisted with each and every kind." "<b>The work of a חֵשֶׁב.</b> I have already explained that it is a weaving of two walls, such that the patterns of its two sides are not alike."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:55:15.209415802Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}